    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field so that creating an owner records the number of pre-existing owners sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName` (case-insensitive), and that count is returned on reads.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 added a persisted `namesake_count` column with a `namesakeCount` field plus getter/setter, mirroring how `customerCode`/`householdId` are stored.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** (`addOwner`) \u2014 before saving the new owner, set `namesakeCount` via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper that counts existing owners (from `findAllOwners()`, i.e. before the insert) whose first and last names match case-insensitively. Since the count is taken pre-save, it never includes the new owner itself.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it (MapStruct maps it by name automatically) and it appears in the API response.\n\n4. **Schema DDL** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` (two owners sharing \"Ann <surname>\", the second expecting `namesakeCount == 1`) passes, and the full suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS` with no failures.",
